# Occupancy in three dimensions

A dense three-dimensional grid of $0.1$ m voxels over a city block of $200 \times 200 \times 20$ m holds $8 \times 10^8$ cells, almost all of them empty or unknown. OctoMap [](cite:hornung2013) stores the same map in an [octree](../neighbour-search/#octrees), each node holding the log-odds of the region it covers. Its leaves are voxels at the finest resolution, but a node whose eight children have all reached the same clamped value is _pruned_: its children are deleted and it stands for all of them. Large free regions and the solid interiors of buildings therefore cost a few nodes each, and the map can be queried at any coarser resolution by stopping the descent early. Each inner node also stores the maximum occupancy of its children, so that a query for whether a large region is free, as a planner checking a path makes, can be answered without descending to the leaves.

Driving systems more often use maps of two or two and a half dimensions. A bird's-eye occupancy grid records, for each ground cell, whether it is free, occupied or unknown, for planning on the road. An _elevation map_ records the height of the terrain in each cell, and its slope and roughness, for vehicles that leave the road. Both are updated with the same log-odds and the same ray traversal, projected onto the ground plane.

# Removing moving objects

A point map built by stacking registered scans keeps everything the sensor saw, including cars and pedestrians that were moving, which appear as long smeared trails of points, _ghosts_, through the map. A map meant for localisation or planning must not contain them, and occupancy reasoning removes them: a point on a moving object occupies a cell that later beams pass through, so the cell's log-odds fall, and points in cells that end up free are deleted. The figure above shows this in miniature, as the pedestrian's path is first marked occupied and then cleared.

Ray casting through every cell for every scan is expensive, and many map-cleaning methods work in the range image instead, a check called _visibility_ or _free-space carving_: map points are projected into the range image of each scan, and a map point that projects onto a pixel whose measured range is clearly greater, so that the beam passed through the place where the point should have been, is marked dynamic. Grazing angles and the sparsity of distant pixels cause errors in both directions, and practical methods combine the check across many scans, at several resolutions, and with ground segmentation to avoid deleting the ground under a vehicle.

# Signed distance fields

Occupancy is not the only volumetric quantity a map can store. A _truncated signed distance field_ (TSDF) stores in each voxel the distance along the beam to the nearest observed surface, positive in front of it and negative behind, truncated to a band near the surface and averaged over observations with weights. The surface is the zero crossing of the field, which can be extracted as a mesh by the marching cubes algorithm, and its interpolation gives surfaces far smoother than the voxel size. TSDFs, introduced for depth cameras, are used for LiDAR surface reconstruction and for maps from which planners need distances to obstacles. They are updated by the same ray traversal, restricted to the truncation band around each return.

# Summary

An occupancy grid stores for each cell the probability that it is occupied, updated with each beam by adding the inverse sensor model in log-odds [](eq:log-odds): occupied at the endpoint, free along the way, untouched beyond, with clamping to keep the map responsive. Amanatides–Woo traversal visits exactly the cells each beam crosses at a cost proportional to their number, and octrees with pruning make three-dimensional occupancy maps compact. The same reasoning about free space removes moving objects from point maps. The next writeup turns to those moving objects themselves: following them over time with multi-object tracking.
